{"body":"## The check\n\nFor every record carrying both a `cas_rn` and a CHEBI `ontology_id`, ask which\nCHEBI term ChEBI itself registers that CAS against:\n\n```\nCAS -> CHEBI index from chebi.db (oio:hasDbXref, 29,736 CAS numbers)\n\nrecords with CAS + CHEBI id        1370\n  CAS registered on THIS term      1074\n  CAS not in ChEBI at all           206\n  CAS registered on a DIFFERENT term  90   <- defect candidates\n```\n\n**This class is invisible to InChIKey comparison**, which is why it survived the\nCAS-vs-structure checking done so far: the mis-mapped term is usually a\nparent/grouping class with no structure at all, so there is no key to compare.\nThe failure mode is a name lookup landing on a parent while the CAS points at\nthe specific child.\n\n## Cross-validation\n\nThe check independently rediscovers four defects found separately, one at a time,\nby the research lane — before this query existed:\n\n| record | CURIE | CAS points to |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Ca-pantothenate` | CHEBI:29032 `(R)-pantothenate` | **CHEBI:31345 Calcium pantothenate** (the #315 merge) |\n| `02_Thiamine_Pyrophosphate` | CHEBI:9532 | CHEBI:18290 `…diphosphate chloride` |\n| `Bicine` | CHEBI:39065 (structureless class) | CHEBI:40957 `N,N-bis(2-hydroxyethyl)glycine` |\n| `Aluminium sulfate` | CHEBI:74772 (class) | CHEBI:74768 `aluminium sulfate (anhydrous)` |\n| `Bisabolene` | CHEBI:49235 (family class) | CHEBI:49240 `alpha-bisabolene` |\n\nFour separate agents reaching five of these independently, and a mechanical query\nreproducing all of them, is good evidence the remaining 85 are real.\n\n## Two sub-classes, different remedies — do not mass-apply\n\n**(a) CURIE is a parent; the label names the specific child.** Fix the CURIE.\n`Bicine` (a structureless grouping class with a disjunctive definition),\n`Bisabolene` (family over 16 terms — note `C15H24` is shared by all of them, so\na formula check passes on the wrong term), `Ca-pantothenate`, `Aluminium sulfate`,\n`alpha-Lactose` → `Alpha-lactose monohydrate`, `Carboxymethyl cellulose` →\n`carboxymethylcellulose sodium`.\n\n**(b) CURIE is correctly generic for a generic label; the *CAS* is over-specific.**\nFix or drop the CAS, not the mapping. `Arginine` (CHEBI:29016, stereo-agnostic —\ncorrect for a bare label) whose CAS 74-79-3 is L-arginine's; `Ascorbic acid`\n(CHEBI:22652) with L-ascorbic acid's CAS; `D-Ribose` with D-ribofuranose's.\n\nSub-class (b) is the same shape as the bare-anion convention in #319: a\ndeliberately generic term is right for a generic label, and it is the\n*over-specific companion field* that is wrong. Getting this backwards would\nbreak correct records.\n\n## Suggested action\n\nTriage the 90 into (a) and (b); the discriminator is whether the record's own\nlabel is generic or specific. Then add the query as a standing check — it is\ncheap, needs only `chebi.db`, and catches a class nothing else does.\n\nAlso worth noting the 206 \"CAS not in ChEBI at all\": not a defect by itself, but\nit is the population where a wrong CAS can hide indefinitely, since neither this\ncheck nor an InChIKey comparison can adjudicate it.\n\n## Provenance\n\nProposed by the Claude research lane after it hit the same shape three times\n(`Bicine`, `Bisabolene`, `02_Thiamine_Pyrophosphate`) and observed that InChIKey\ncomparison structurally cannot catch it. Verified repo-wide against chebi.db.\nRelated: #315, #316, #317, #319.","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDORhCjFc8AAAABN5aKGw","author":{"login":"realmarcin"},"authorAssociation":"MEMBER","body":"## A third remedy pattern: the CAS belongs on a *sibling record*\n\nWorked example from the carboxymethyl cellulose pair, which sits in the 90:\n\n```\nCHEBI:85146      Carboxymethyl cellulose               EXACT_MATCH   cas 9004-32-4\nFOODON:03460374  Carboxymethyl cellulose (sodium salt) LEXICAL_MATCH cas (none)\n```\n\n`9004-32-4` is registered in ChEBI against `CHEBI:234035 carboxymethylcellulose\nsodium` — hence the #320 flag. But neither sub-class (a) nor (b) is the right fix:\n\n- The CHEBI:85146 mapping is **correct** — the term denotes exactly what that\n  record's label denotes. So not sub-class (a).\n- The CAS is not merely over-specific for a generic label — it belongs to a\n  *different substance that this repo already has a record for*. So not\n  sub-class (b) either.\n\nThe fix is to **move the CAS to the sibling record**, where it is the correct\nvalue. And that sibling has its own defect: `FOODON:03460374` is labelled\n`carboxymethyl cellulose` — no sodium — so it drops the one thing its label\nspecifies. A better term was resolved: **`NCIT:C83596 Carboxymethylcellulose\nSodium`**, label-exact for the salt *and* carrying CAS 9004-32-4.\n\nSo one #320 hit resolves into: keep mapping A, re-ground record B, and move the\nCAS from A to B.\n\n**These two are explicitly *not* duplicates** and should not be merged — free\npolymer acid and sodium salt are different substances. Noting that because I had\nguessed they were a merge candidate when handing them to the lane; the research\nrefuted it. Worth being careful with the analogous pairs in #315: the\npantothenate/glutamate/tartrate cases *are* merges, this one is not, and the\ndiscriminator is whether the two labels denote the same substance or a\nsubstance and its salt.","createdAt":"2026-08-08T18:47:19Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/320#issuecomment-5227579931","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDORhCjFc8AAAABOqUXBQ","author":{"login":"realmarcin"},"authorAssociation":"MEMBER","body":"## 48 of the 84 are not defects — they are CAS registry granularity\n\nThe check asks whether a record's CAS is registered by ChEBI against a *different* term than the record's CURIE. That question conflates two very different situations, separable with one query: **is the CAS's term a descendant of the record's term in ChEBI's entailed hierarchy?**\n\n| relationship | count | reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| CAS's term is a **descendant** of the record's | **48** | CAS is finer-grained than a deliberately generic term — expected |\n| CAS's term is an **ancestor** of the record's | 3 | the CURIE is over-specific for the label |\n| CAS's term is **unrelated** | 33 | genuine conflict |\n\nThe 48 are the `Arginine` → `CHEBI:29016 arginine`, CAS `74-79-3` → `L-arginine` shape: `Ascorbic acid`→`L-ascorbic acid`, `D-Ribose`→`D-ribofuranose`, `D-Mannose`→`D-mannopyranose`, `Anethole`→`trans-anethole`, `caffeic acid`→`trans-caffeic acid`. In each the record's label matches the generic term *exactly*, and §3 step 4 makes that term correct. The CAS registry simply has no number for a stereo- or tautomer-unspecified class — `74-79-3` is L-arginine because that is the one that occurs biologically. **Nothing is wrong with these records.**\n\nThis also explains why the earlier note in #338 (\"neither the label-specificity nor the structureless-class heuristic works alone\") kept failing: 23 of the 84 sit on structureless class terms, but structurelessness cuts across all three rows of the table, so it cannot be the discriminator. Ancestry can.\n\n## The 3 ancestor cases — CURIE over-specific\n\n    Dextrose             CHEBI:4167  D-glucopyranose      CAS -> D-glucose\n    maltose              CHEBI:18167 alpha-maltose        CAS -> maltose\n    N-Acetyl-L-glutamine CHEBI:21553 N-acetyl-L-glutamine CAS -> N(2)-acetylglutamine\n\n## The 33 unrelated cases, grouped\n\n**CURIE is wrong, and ChEBI has the right term** (highest confidence — the label names it unambiguously):\n- `m-Inositol` → `CHEBI:10642 scyllo-inositol`, CAS `87-89-8` → **myo-inositol**. \"m-Inositol\" *is* myo-inositol; scyllo- is a different stereoisomer.\n- `NiSO4 x 6 H2O` → `CHEBI:53001 nickel sulfate`, CAS `10101-97-0` → **nickel sulfate hexahydrate**. §3 step 1 is available and unused.\n- `Sodium succinate dibasic` → `CHEBI:15741 succinic acid` — the record is the sodium salt, mapped to the free acid.\n\n**CAS is wrong; the term is right** — mostly the #334 scrambled-hydrate family, where the CAS denotes a different hydration state than the label:\n`CaCl2 x 6 H2O` (CAS is anhydrous), `Na2S x 9 H2O` (anhydrous), `Na2SeO3` (pentahydrate), `NiCl2 x 2 H2O` and `x 5 H2O` (hexahydrate), `NiSO4 x 7 H2O` (hexahydrate), `Na2MoO4 x 7 H2O` (dihydrate), `Sulfur (powder)` (CAS `7783-06-4` is hydrogen sulfide).\n\n**Protonation / oxidation state** — overlaps #319 and should be decided with it, not separately: `EDTA` → `EDTA(2-)`, `3-Hydroxypropionate` → anion vs acid, `Malate` → anion vs acid, `DL-carnitine` → `carnitinium`, `Cobalamine` → `cob(III)alamin` vs `cob(I)alamin`.\n\n**Salt / hydrate form of the CAS**: `Berberine`→berberine chloride, `Carboxymethyl cellulose`→CMC sodium, `Meropenem`→trihydrate, `alpha-Lactose`→monohydrate, `Sodium Citrate`→dihydrate, `Mg-citrate`→trimagnesium dicitrate.\n\n**Needs investigation**: `D` → `CHEBI:29958 L-aspartic acid residue` with CAS `138-86-3` (limonene). The label is a single character — almost certainly a parsing artifact upstream, and neither the CURIE nor the CAS should be trusted.\n\n## Suggested retitle\n\n\"90 records whose CAS-RN is registered against a different term\" overstates it. **36** are candidates (3 + 33); 48 are correct records where the CAS is simply more specific than a generic term. Adding the ancestry test to the detector would stop it reporting the 48 on every run.","createdAt":"2026-08-13T10:03:26Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/320#issuecomment-5278865157","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDORhCjFc8AAAABOwzuKg","author":{"login":"realmarcin"},"authorAssociation":"MEMBER","body":"## Resolved — every flagged record is now either benign or verified correct\n\n    flagged            84  ->  55\n      benign               49   CAS finer-grained than a deliberately generic term\n      genuine candidates   36  ->  6\n\nAnd **all 6 remaining are verified correct**, not open work:\n\n| record | term | CAS points to | why it stays |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Malate` | malate | malic acid | definition says \"deprotonation of **at least one**\" — deliberately agnostic |\n| `3-Hydroxypropionate` | 3-hydroxypropionate | the acid | the **label** names the anion |\n| `DL-carnitine` | carnitine | carnitinium | definition is structural, silent on protonation |\n| `phosphorous acid` | phosphorous acid | phosphonic acid | the IUPAC name for the same compound |\n| `N-Acetyl-L-glutamine` | N-acetyl-L-glutamine | N(2)-acetylglutamine | the CAS's term is the stereo-**unspecified** ancestor |\n| `Thiamine pyrophosphate` | thiamine(1+) diphosphate | the (1-) conjugate base | a charge state — #319's rule, not #320's |\n\nThese stay flagged only because ChEBI doesn't model *conjugate-base-of* as subsumption, exactly as it doesn't model hydrate-of (#342). The detector can't distinguish them from real conflicts without that.\n\n## How it was resolved\n\n**The ancestry test** — is the CAS's term a *descendant* of the record's? — separated 48 (now 49) non-defects from genuine conflicts in one query, and explained why the heuristics tried in #338 kept failing: 23 of the 84 sit on structureless terms, but structurelessness cuts across all three ancestry classes.\n\n**The genuine conflicts split three ways:**\n\n- **Duplicates shadowing a correctly-mapped sibling** (#347) — `m-Inositol` was on *scyllo*-inositol, a different stereoisomer, via a `CAS_RN_LOOKUP` its own CAS contradicts. Also `NiSO4 x 6 H2O`, `maltose`, `Dextrose`, `glucose`. The CAS conflict was a *symptom* of the duplicate.\n- **Wrong CAS** (#347) — 7 scrambled hydrate CAS numbers, and 5 naming a different compound entirely (`Sulfur (powder)` carried **hydrogen sulfide**, a gas).\n- **Over-specific term** (#350, #351) — `Cobalamine` on the +3 oxidation state, `EDTA` on the (2-) anion, `Thiamine pyrophosphate` on the chloride salt.\n- **CAS names a more specific form** (#353) — resolved by the curation decision that the CAS governs for salts, hydrates and racemates: `Berberine`→chloride, `alpha-Lactose`→monohydrate, `Kawain`→DL-kavain, and 5 others.\n\n## Suggested follow-up\n\nFold the ancestry test into the detector so the 49 stop being reported, and add a conjugate-base-of / tautomer-of exclusion so the 6 above stop being re-triaged. Otherwise this issue regenerates its own backlog on every run.","createdAt":"2026-08-13T19:53:07Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/320#issuecomment-5285670442","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDORhCjFc8AAAABOwzu1g","author":{"login":"realmarcin"},"authorAssociation":"MEMBER","body":"Closing: 84 flagged -> 55, of which 49 are benign CAS granularity and the remaining 6 are verified correct under #319's protonation rule. All genuine conflicts resolved across #347, #350, #351 and #353. Follow-up to make the detector stop reporting the benign classes is noted above.","createdAt":"2026-08-13T19:53:08Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/320#issuecomment-5285670614","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":320,"state":"CLOSED","title":"90 records whose CAS-RN is registered in ChEBI against a different term than their CURIE","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/320"}
